CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is explicit, but actual user-visible impact requires a browser client controlled by the new worker and runtime matching asset requests, cache contents, preload activity, or component cleanup. Those conditions are not established by the supplied diff.

The web client adds a service worker that intercepts asset image GETs, caches successful responses by URL and deployment version, accepts preload and cancellation messages, and removes older CacheStorage entries during activation. Photo viewing and thumbnail components now coordinate image work through that messaging channel. A production-stack shutdown Make target was also added, while some end-to-end UI assertions were removed.

Likely behavioral changes
  • For clients controlled by the new service worker, matching original and thumbnail asset GETs are served from a deployment-versioned browser cache when present; otherwise they are fetched and successful 200 responses are stored for later requests.
  • Adjacent photo preloading now requests service-worker-managed loading through a broadcast message instead of directly creating an image load in the viewer.
  • Destroying a photo loader or thumbnail now sends a cancellation request; if the worker has a pending load under the same normalized URL, its fetch is aborted.
  • Operators can now stop the production Docker Compose stack and remove orphaned containers with the new prod-down Make target.

Investigate
  • Trace how the service-worker entry is built, registered, updated, and scoped, and confirm which matching asset API requests it actually controls.
  • Determine whether asset responses are identity- or permission-dependent and whether cache entries remain safe across login, logout, account switching, sharing modes, and authorization changes.
  • Trace all producers and consumers of the immich BroadcastChannel protocol and model multiple components requesting or cancelling the same URL concurrently.
  • Review cache ownership across the origin and deployment lifecycle before allowing activation to remove non-current cache names.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not establish whether this SvelteKit service-worker file is automatically registered, what URL scope it receives, or when it begins controlling existing clients.
  • The complete set of navigable web routes that instantiate the modified photo viewer or thumbnail component is not shown.
  • Whether users observe cache hits, preloads, or cancellations depends on a controlling service worker, matching asset traffic, component destruction timing, and the current browser cache state.
  • The diff does not establish whether asset response bytes or authorization vary between users, permission changes, shared-link contexts, or other server-side state for an otherwise identical URL.
  • The supplied code does not establish all browser-specific BroadcastChannel and service-worker MessageEvent semantics relevant to message delivery and use of event.origin as the base for relative URLs.

